## A glance at the numbers
This year has been notably busy in the realm of food recalls. So far, we've seen a whopping **714 recalls**, with **181 classified as Class I**, indicating a reasonable probability that the use of or exposure to the recalled product will cause serious adverse health consequences or death. If this isn't enough to put one off their lunch, consider this: nearly **88 companies** have been repeat offenders this year.

## This month's latest
The most recent recall, as of June 24, 2026, features **MID DOLCIARIA SRL** for their gluten-free cannoli. The reason? An unexpected appearance of unapproved artificial colors, chlorophyll E140 and E141. While "greenwashing" takes on a literal meaning, consumers should steer clear of these treats.

Other notable recalls include:

- **Al Yaman Halawa Extra Pistachio**: A pistachio halva potentially contaminated with Salmonella.

- **Jonco Industries White Cheddar Seasoning**: Recalled due to the use of contaminated milk powder.

- **Fairhope Roasting Company's Cold Brew Coffee Concentrate**: Mislabeling led to missing "Keep Refrigerated" instructions.

## Repeat Offenders: A Persistent Pattern
Considering the repeat offenders list for 2026, one name rings out loud and clear: **Moonlight Packing Corporation**, with 11 recalls--every single one a Class I. This performance earns them the dubious honor of the lowest grade among the top offending brands, a solid "F". Close on their heels is **Ghirardelli Chocolate Company**, with 10 recalls, all Class I.

## The Big Picture
Salmonella remains the most recurring hazard this year, with **178 incidents**--each a potential threat to public health. Mislabeling, while not life-threatening, disrupts consumer trust and continues to make an appearance. If you've purchased food recently, it's worth double-checking those labels and keeping track of recall news.

Stay vigilant, and until next time, keep your shelves safe.
